# Architecture

Oriel separates platform-neutral application logic from operating-system-specific implementations.

| Layer | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `src/core/App.zig` | Cross-platform window, webview, asset serving, IPC dispatch, events, dev mode |
| `src/core/ipc.zig` | Command and event marshaling, TypeScript type generation |
| `src/core/security.zig` | Navigation policy, IPC capabilities, CSP enforcement |
| `src/platform/platform.zig` | Compile-time platform selection (Linux, Windows, macOS) |
| `src/platform/<os>/` | OS-specific shell: window creation, webview engine, IPC transport, dev server |
| `src/modules/` | Built-in: tray, updater, SQL, media server, notifications, etc. (default on, opt-out) |
| `src/plugins/` | App-specific: global shortcuts, input injection, clipboard (opt-in) |

Each module and plugin can have platform-specific backends (e.g. `clipboard.zig` facade + `clipboard/{linux,windows}.zig` implementations).

# Commands and IPC

The Zig side defines a `Commands` struct with public functions. Each function becomes an IPC endpoint:

```zig
pub const Commands = struct {
    pub fn greet(gpa: std.mem.Allocator, args: struct { name: []const u8 }) ![]const u8 {
        return std.fmt.allocPrint(gpa, "Hello, {s}!", .{args.name});
    }
};
```

The TypeScript side gets a typed `invoke` function:

```ts
const msg = await invoke("greet", { name: "Ada" });
```

## Synchronous vs. asynchronous

By default, commands run on the main thread. To avoid blocking the UI:

```zig
pub const Commands = struct {
    pub const async_commands = .{ "slow_command" };

    pub fn slow_command(gpa: std.mem.Allocator, io: std.Io) ![]const u8 {
        // Runs on worker thread pool; UI remains responsive
        // (optional: receive std.Io for network, file I/O, etc.)
        return "done";
    }
};
```

Async commands receive their own arena allocator (freed after the reply). Each command invocation gets a worker thread from the pool; replies are sent back on the main thread.

# Events

Events flow from Zig to JavaScript. Declare them in `Events`:

```zig
pub const Events = struct {
    notes_changed: []const Note,
    status_updated: struct { message: []const u8, progress: u32 },
};
const events = oriel.App.events(Events);
```

Emit from anywhere (any thread):

```zig
events.emit(.notes_changed, notes);
events.emit(.status_updated, .{ .message = "Saving...", .progress = 50 });
```

Listen in TypeScript:

```ts
listen("notes_changed", (notes) => setNotes(notes));
```

Events are type-checked on both sides. Targeted events can be sent to specific windows with `window.emitTo(label, event, data)`.

# Security model

Oriel follows Tauri's security architecture.

## Navigation policy

The webview is confined to:
- `app://app` (embedded assets, only in production)
- The Vite dev server (`http://localhost:5173`, only in dev builds)
- Origins listed in `Config.security.allowed_origins` (read-only, no IPC)

All other navigation (iframes, redirects, popups, links) is blocked. External http(s)/mailto/tel links open in the system browser via `openExternal`.

## Command capabilities

Apps own all commands by default. Remote origins (e.g. a page from `https://docs.example.com`) must be granted specific commands via capabilities:

```zig
.security = .{
    .capabilities = &.{
        .{ .origin = "https://docs.example.com", .commands = &.{ "greet" } },
    },
}
```

Capabilities are matched against the exact origin of the currently-loaded page. Wildcards are supported in the origin (`https://*.example.com`).

## Content Security Policy

Every `app://` response includes a strict CSP header:

```
default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:;
```

No inline scripts, no `eval`. Dev builds (from the Vite server) set no CSP.

## Bridge injection

The `window.oriel` object is only injected on pages allowed to use IPC. Remote origins without the `oriel` command capability cannot call anything.

# Windows and multi-window apps

Open windows from Zig:

```zig
const win = try oriel.App.openWindow(.{
    .label = "settings",
    .title = "Settings",
    .url = "/settings",
    .width = 600,
    .height = 400,
});
```

Or from JavaScript (requires the `window` capability):

```ts
import { window } from "./oriel";

const child = await window.open({
    label: "child",
    url: "/child",
    title: "Child Window",
    width: 400,
    height: 300,
});

await child.setTitle("Updated");
await child.close();
```

Access the current window and query others:

```ts
const current = window.current();     // WindowHandle for this window
const all = await window.all();       // all open windows
const specific = await window.get("settings");  // by label

// Targeted events
await window.emitTo("settings", "config_update", { theme: "dark" });
```

Windows can emit events to all windows:

```ts
import { emit } from "./oriel";
emit("global_update", { version: "1.2.0" });
```

## Window security

Window creation is opt-in and scoped:
- App-local origins can open windows by default
- Remote origins are blocked unless granted the `window` capability
- Only app-local URLs are allowed by default; remote URLs must pass `allowed_origins`
- Dangerous schemes (`javascript:`, `file:`, `data:`) are always rejected
- Labels are validated (alphanumeric, underscores, dashes; max 64 chars)
- Windows can only modify themselves by default; `allow_modify_other_windows` allows otherwise
- Cap the concurrent window count with `max_windows` (default 16)

# Routes and Single-Page Apps

In `WindowOptions.url`, relative paths like `"/settings"` are resolved to:
- **Dev mode:** the Vite server (e.g. `http://localhost:5173/settings`)
- **Production:** embedded assets (`app://app/settings` on Linux, `https://app.localhost/settings` on Windows)

Absolute URLs with schemes are checked against `allowed_origins` and `capabilities`.

## SPA Fallback caveat

With `config.spa_fallback = true` (default), deep links and 404s serve `index.html` for client-side routers. However, paths whose last segment contains a dot (e.g. `/users/john.doe` or `/report.pdf`) are treated as asset files and will return 404 if not found.

This is intentional: it prevents `/api/data.json` from loading `index.html` when the API endpoint doesn't exist.

# External links

The `openExternal(url)` function opens a URL in the user's default application:

```ts
import { openExternal } from "./oriel";
await openExternal("https://ziglang.org");
```

Only `http:`, `https:` and `mailto:` schemes are allowed by default (configurable). Dangerous schemes (`file:`, `javascript:`, `data:`, `blob:`, `about:`) and control characters are always rejected.

On Linux, it uses the XDG desktop portal or `xdg-open`. On Windows, it uses `ShellExecuteW`.
